   "source": [
    "## Exercise 1 - Starter-motor fault\n",
    "\n",
    "Here the headlights are not dim, so the battery hypothesis fails. The engine does not crank and there is a clicking sound, therefore the starter-motor rule succeeds."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Checking hypothesis: Battery Fault\n",
      "  Are the headlights dim? no\n",
      "Checking hypothesis: Starter Motor Fault\n",
      "  Do you hear a clicking sound when starting? yes\n",
      "  Is the engine not cranking? yes\n",
      "Diagnosis: Starter Motor Fault\n",
      "Questions asked: ['Dim Headlights', 'Clicking Sound', 'Engine Not Cranking']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "starter_case = BackwardChainingDiagnoser(scripted_answers('no', 'yes', 'yes'))\n",
    "result = starter_case.diagnose()\n",
    "print('Questions asked:', [readable(q) for q in starter_case.questions_asked])\n",
    "assert result == 'starter_motor_fault'\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 2 - Effect of hypothesis order\n",
    "\n",
    "For the same evidence, testing `starter_motor_fault` first asks only its two relevant facts. With the default order, the engine first asks about dim headlights while attempting the battery hypothesis. Thus, backward chaining is goal-directed, but the chosen goal order controls the questioning path."
   ]
  },
